An expectation of boons and blessings. Things believed to be true before there is
evidence, written down so that when the evidence arrives it is clear which
beliefs it confirmed and which it demolished.

--

I believe that computing sight for security reasons will turn out to have been
the right way to compute it for drawing reasons too, and that this will keep
happening. The visibility polygon is the first case: it was required by the
refusal to send what a viewer cannot see, and it happens to be exactly what a
renderer needs for a clean shadow edge. When correctness and beauty agree, it is
usually because the decision was right for a reason nobody has written down yet.

I believe one record for everything that stands in the space will hold, and that
the first time somebody wants to break it -- a props array, a separate creature
type -- will be the first time the design has gone wrong somewhere else.

I believe the control dial is two facts and not four, and that the tavern's
commander will require no code that a player driving one body did not already
require. This is the project's most testable belief and phase 6 is the test.

I believe fixed point will feel like an inconvenience for about a week and then
never be thought about again, and that the first person to propose "just use
floats, it is easier" will be proposing an hour-long replay divergence that
nobody will ever find.

I believe the port range will be the thing that stops somebody from joining, and
that it will happen on the first evening a real table tries this, and that the
reasons written down for one port per participant will look considerably weaker
at that moment than they do now.

I believe six people will find something the security model did not anticipate
within an hour, and that it will be social rather than technical -- somebody
inferring where the monster is from how the light moves, which is not a leak and
is the best thing that could possibly happen.

I believe the demos will be the part of this that survives. Not the server.
